│     └─0 text "Rule testing: fixtures and snapshots"
├─62  paragraph[9]
│     ├─0 text "To test rules, Ruff uses the mdtest framework, initially developed for ty. Mdtests are written as\nMarkdown files with Python code and TOML configuration blocks alongside prose\ndescriptions. Generally, there will be one directory per linter (e.g. "
│     ├─1 inlineCode "flake8-bandit"
│     ├─2 text " for the "
│     ├─3 inlineCode "S"
│     ├─4 text "\nrules) with nested files for each rule (e.g. "
│     ├─5 inlineCode "unsafe-markup-use.md"
│     ├─6 text " for "
│     ├─7 inlineCode "S704"
│     └─8 text "). Within these\nfiles, you can define additional Markdown sections to group related tests and their settings\ntogether."
├─63  paragraph[13]
│     ├─0  text "You can see "
│     ├─1  link[1]
│     │    │ title: null
│     │    │ url: "https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff/blob/main/crates/ty_test/README.md"
│     │    └─0 text "the ty_test\nREADME"
│     ├─2  text " for a full description\nof the options supported by mdtest, but in general, you should use "
│     ├─3  inlineCode "# error"
│     ├─4  text " comments to assert that\na particular rule fires on a given line. You can instead use "
│     ├─5  inlineCode "# snapshot"
│     ├─6  text " comments to also generate\nan inline snapshot of the resulting diagnostic. You can automatically accept these snapshot changes\nby setting the "
│     ├─7  inlineCode "MDTEST_UPDATE_SNAPSHOTS=1"
│     ├─8  text " environment variable when running your tests. Prefer\nusing "
│     ├─9  inlineCode "# error"
│     ├─10 text " for the bulk of your assertions because it helps to keep the tests short, but\n"
│     ├─11 inlineCode "# snapshot"
│     └─12 text " can be used to capture details of the diagnostic or suggested fix, when applicable."
├─64  paragraph[5]
│     ├─0 text "For example, a minimal mdtest for "
│     ├─1 inlineCode "module-import-not-at-top-of-file"
│     ├─2 text " ("
│     ├─3 inlineCode "E402"
│     └─4 text ") would look something\nlike this:"
├─65  code "<!-- crates/ruff_linter/resources/mdtest/pycodestyle/module-import-not-at-top-of-file.md -->\n\n# `module-import-not-at-top-of-file` (`E402`)\n\n## Basic examples\n\n```toml\nlint.select = [\"E402\"]\n```\n\nThis is an example of code flagged by the rule:\n\n```py\na = 1\nimport os  # snapshot: module-import-not-at-top-of-file\n```\n\n```snapshot\nerror[E402]: Module level import not at top of file\n --> src/mdtest_snippet.py:2:1\n  |\n2 | import os  # snapshot: module-import-not-at-top-of-file\n  | ^^^^^^^^^\n  |\n```\n\nAdditional cases can just use `# error` since the diagnostics should look the same:\n\n```py\nb = 2\nimport something_else  # error: [module-import-not-at-top-of-file]\n```\n\n## More complicated configuration\n\nIf any of your tests require special configuration options, you can define additional TOML code\nblocks. These blocks accept all of the configuration options that Ruff itself does:\n\n\n```toml\ntarget-version = \"py310\"\n\n[lint]\nselect = [\"E\"]\n\n[lint.pycodestyle]\nmax-line-length = 100\n```"
│       lang: "markdown"
│       meta: null
├─66  paragraph[5]
│     ├─0 text "Mdtests are run as part of a normal "
│     ├─1 inlineCode "cargo test"
│     ├─2 text " invocation as part of the "
│     ├─3 inlineCode "ruff_mdtest"
│     └─4 text " crate."
├─67  heading[1]
│     │ depth: 3
│     └─0 text "Example: Adding an auto-fix"
├─68  paragraph[9]
│     ├─0 text "Sometimes a lint violation has a natural fix in the form of an edit to the\nsource code. To surface this suggestion to the user, you will need to attach\na "
│     ├─1 inlineCode "Fix"
│     ├─2 text " to the diagnostic using one of the helper methods on "
│     ├─3 inlineCode "DiagnosticGuard"
│     ├─4 text " found in "
│     ├─5 inlineCode "crates/ruff_linter/src/checkers/ast/mod.rs"
│     ├─6 text " (e.g. "
│     ├─7 inlineCode "set_fix"
│     └─8 text ")."
├─69  paragraph[5]
│     ├─0 text "You will also need to decide when to offer this fix\nand whether it is safe or unsafe. Please refer to the documentation on\n"
│     ├─1 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/linter/#fix-safety"
│     │   └─0 text "fix safety"
│     ├─2 text " to determine\nwhether to offer a safe or unsafe fix. If a fix is (sometimes) unsafe,\nupdate the rule's documentation with an explanation under the heading\n"
│     ├─3 inlineCode "## Fix safety"
│     └─4 text "."
├─70  paragraph[3]
│     ├─0 text "Often the nontrivial work lies in generating\nthe new source code in the form of an "
│     ├─1 inlineCode "Edit"
│     └─2 text ".\nThere are three main ways to do this:"
├─71  list[3]
│     │ ordered: true
│     │ start: 1
│     │ spread: false
│     ├─0 listItem[1]
│     │   │ spread: false
│     │   │ checked: null
│     │   └─0 paragraph[4]
│     │       ├─0 strong[1]
│     │       │   └─0 text "AST-based edits"
│     │       ├─1 text ". Here we construct the AST node that we wish\nthe new source code to parse to, and then generate the text using a method on\n"
│     │       ├─2 inlineCode "checker.generator()"
│     │       └─3 text ". The benefit of such edits is that they should essentially\nnever introduce syntax errors and they will have predictable formatting. On the\nother hand, it can be cumbersome to build an AST node by hand, and one has less\nfine-grained control over comments."
│     ├─1 listItem[1]
│     │   │ spread: false
│     │   │ checked: null
│     │   └─0 paragraph[2]
│     │       ├─0 strong[1]
│     │       │   └─0 text "CST-based edits"
│     │       └─1 text ". This is similar to\nthe above except that one leverages LibCST to first parse the source\ninto a concrete syntax tree and then modifies it as needed. This\nretains more of the formatting of the original source while retaining\nthe other benefits of AST-based edits. However, it introduces\noverhead."
│     └─2 listItem[1]
│         │ spread: false
│         │ checked: null
│         └─0 paragraph[2]
│             ├─0 strong[1]
│             │   └─0 text "Text-based edits"
│             └─1 text ". Here we directly construct the replacement\ntext as a string. This gives you the most control over what the\nedit will look like, and is often more performant. However,\nit can be much more difficult to ensure that the fix does not\nintroduce syntax errors, especially on unusual source code.\nIf you adopt this approach, be sure to add even more test fixtures\nthan usual."
