├─81  heading[1]
│     │ depth: 3
│     └─0 text "Opening a PR"
├─82  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "After you finish your changes, the next step is to open a PR. By default, two\nsections will be filled into the PR body: the summary and the test plan."
├─83  heading[1]
│     │ depth: 4
│     └─0 text "The summary"
├─84  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "The summary is intended to give us as maintainers information about your PR.\nThis should typically include a link to the relevant issue(s) you're addressing\nin your PR, as well as a summary of the issue and your approach to fixing it. If\nyou have any questions about your approach or design, or if you considered\nalternative approaches, that can also be helpful to include."
├─85  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "AI can be helpful in generating both the code and summary of your PR, but a\nsuccessful contribution should still be carefully reviewed by you and the\nsummary editorialized before submitting a PR. A great summary is thorough but\nalso succinct and gives us the context we need to review your PR."
├─86  paragraph[3]
│     ├─0 text "You can find examples of excellent issues and PRs by searching for the\n"
│     ├─1 link[1]
│     │   │ title: null
│     │   │ url: "https://github.com/astral-sh/ruff/issues?q=label%3A%22great%20writeup%22"
│     │   └─0 inlineCode "great writeup"
│     └─2 text "\nlabel."
├─87  heading[1]
│     │ depth: 4
│     └─0 text "The test plan"
├─88  paragraph[3]
│     ├─0 text "The test plan is likely to be shorter than the summary and can be as simple as\n\"Added new snapshot tests for "
│     ├─1 inlineCode "RUF123"
│     └─2 text ",\" at least for rule bugs. For LSP or some\ntypes of CLI changes, in particular, it can also be helpful to include\nscreenshots or recordings of your change in action."
├─89  heading[1]
│     │ depth: 4
│     └─0 text "Ecosystem report"
├─90  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "After opening the PR, an ecosystem report will be run as part of CI. This shows\na diff of linter and formatter behavior before and after the changes in your PR.\nGoing through these changes and reporting your findings in the PR summary or an\nadditional comment help us to review your PR more efficiently. It's also a great\nway to find new test cases to incorporate into your PR if you identify any\nissues."
├─91  heading[1]
│     │ depth: 4
│     └─0 text "PR status"
├─92  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "To help us know when your PR is ready for review again, please either move your\nPR back to a draft while working on it (marking it ready for review afterwards\nwill ping the previous reviewers) or explicitly re-request a review. This helps\nus to avoid re-reviewing a PR while you're still working on it and also to\nprioritize PRs that are definitely ready for review."
├─93  paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "You can also thumbs-up or mark as resolved any comments we leave to let us know\nyou addressed them."
