├─179 heading[1]
│     │ depth: 3
│     └─0 text "Import Categorization"
├─180 paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "To understand Ruff's import categorization system, we first need to define two concepts:"
├─181 list[2]
│     │ ordered: false
│     │ start: null
│     │ spread: false
│     ├─0 listItem[1]
│     │   │ spread: false
│     │   │ checked: null
│     │   └─0 paragraph[9]
│     │       ├─0 text "\"Project root\": The directory containing the "
│     │       ├─1 inlineCode "pyproject.toml"
│     │       ├─2 text ", "
│     │       ├─3 inlineCode "ruff.toml"
│     │       ├─4 text ", or "
│     │       ├─5 inlineCode ".ruff.toml"
│     │       ├─6 text " file,\ndiscovered by identifying the \"closest\" such directory for each Python file. (If you're running\nvia "
│     │       ├─7 inlineCode "ruff --config /path/to/pyproject.toml"
│     │       └─8 text ", then the current working directory is used as the\n\"project root\".)"
│     └─1 listItem[1]
│         │ spread: false
│         │ checked: null
│         └─0 paragraph[5]
│             ├─0 text "\"Package root\": The top-most directory defining the Python package that includes a given Python\nfile. To find the package root for a given Python file, traverse up its parent directories until\nyou reach a parent directory that doesn't contain an "
│             ├─1 inlineCode "__init__.py"
│             ├─2 text " file (and isn't in a subtree\nmarked as a "
│             ├─3 link[1]
│             │   │ title: null
│             │   │ url: "https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/settings/#namespace-packages"
│             │   └─0 text "namespace package"
│             └─4 text "); take the directory\njust before that, i.e., the first directory in the package."
├─182 paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "For example, given:"
├─183 code "my_project\n├── pyproject.toml\n└── src\n    └── foo\n        ├── __init__.py\n        └── bar\n            ├── __init__.py\n            └── baz.py"
│       lang: "text"
│       meta: null
├─184 paragraph[7]
│     ├─0 text "Then when analyzing "
│     ├─1 inlineCode "baz.py"
│     ├─2 text ", the project root would be the top-level directory ("
│     ├─3 inlineCode "./my_project"
│     ├─4 text "),\nand the package root would be "
│     ├─5 inlineCode "./my_project/src/foo"
│     └─6 text "."
├─185 heading[1]
│     │ depth: 4
│     └─0 text "Project root"
├─186 paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "The project root does not have a significant impact beyond that all relative paths within the loaded\nconfiguration file are resolved relative to the project root."
├─187 paragraph[9]
│     ├─0 text "For example, to indicate that "
│     ├─1 inlineCode "bar"
│     ├─2 text " above is a namespace package (it isn't, but let's run with it),\nthe "
│     ├─3 inlineCode "pyproject.toml"
│     ├─4 text " would list "
│     ├─5 inlineCode "namespace-packages = [\"./src/bar\"]"
│     ├─6 text ", which would resolve\nto "
│     ├─7 inlineCode "my_project/src/bar"
│     └─8 text "."
├─188 paragraph[5]
│     ├─0 text "The same logic applies when providing a configuration file via "
│     ├─1 inlineCode "--config"
│     ├─2 text ". In that case, the\n"
│     ├─3 emphasis[1]
│     │   └─0 text "current working directory"
│     └─4 text " is used as the project root, and so all paths in that configuration file\nare resolved relative to the current working directory. (As a general rule, we want to avoid relying\non the current working directory as much as possible, to ensure that Ruff exhibits the same behavior\nregardless of where and how you invoke it — but that's hard to avoid in this case.)"
├─189 paragraph[9]
│     ├─0 text "Additionally, if a "
│     ├─1 inlineCode "pyproject.toml"
│     ├─2 text " file "
│     ├─3 emphasis[1]
│     │   └─0 text "extends"
│     ├─4 text " another configuration file, Ruff will still use\nthe directory containing that "
│     ├─5 inlineCode "pyproject.toml"
│     ├─6 text " file as the project root. For example, if\n"
│     ├─7 inlineCode "./my_project/pyproject.toml"
│     └─8 text " contains:"
├─190 code "[tool.ruff]\nextend = \"/path/to/pyproject.toml\""
│       lang: "toml"
│       meta: null
├─191 paragraph[9]
│     ├─0 text "Then Ruff will use "
│     ├─1 inlineCode "./my_project"
│     ├─2 text " as the project root, even though the configuration file extends\n"
│     ├─3 inlineCode "/path/to/pyproject.toml"
│     ├─4 text ". As such, if the configuration file at "
│     ├─5 inlineCode "/path/to/pyproject.toml"
│     ├─6 text " contains\nany relative paths, they will be resolved relative to "
│     ├─7 inlineCode "./my_project"
│     └─8 text "."
├─192 paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "If a project uses nested configuration files, then Ruff would detect multiple project roots, one for\neach configuration file."
├─193 heading[1]
│     │ depth: 4
│     └─0 text "Package root"
├─194 paragraph[11]
│     ├─0  text "The package root is used to determine a file's \"module path\". Consider, again, "
│     ├─1  inlineCode "baz.py"
│     ├─2  text ". In that\ncase, "
│     ├─3  inlineCode "./my_project/src/foo"
│     ├─4  text " was identified as the package root, so the module path for "
│     ├─5  inlineCode "baz.py"
│     ├─6  text "\nwould resolve to "
│     ├─7  inlineCode "foo.bar.baz"
│     ├─8  text " — as computed by taking the relative path from the package root\n(inclusive of the root itself). The module path can be thought of as \"the path you would use to\nimport the module\" (e.g., "
│     ├─9  inlineCode "import foo.bar.baz"
│     └─10 text ")."
├─195 paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "The package root and module path are used to, e.g., convert relative to absolute imports, and for\nimport categorization, as described below."
├─196 heading[1]
│     │ depth: 4
│     └─0 text "Import categorization"
├─197 paragraph[1]
│     └─0 text "When sorting and formatting import blocks, Ruff categorizes every import into one of five\ncategories:"
├─198 list[5]
│     │ ordered: true
│     │ start: 1
│     │ spread: false
│     ├─0 listItem[1]
│     │   │ spread: false
│     │   │ checked: null
│     │   └─0 paragraph[4]
│     │       ├─0 strong[1]
│     │       │   └─0 text "\"Future\""
│     │       ├─1 text ": the import is a "
│     │       ├─2 inlineCode "__future__"
│     │       └─3 text " import. That's easy: just look at the name of the\nimported module!"
│     ├─1 listItem[1]
│     │   │ spread: false
│     │   │ checked: null
│     │   └─0 paragraph[4]
│     │       ├─0 strong[1]
│     │       │   └─0 text "\"Standard library\""
│     │       ├─1 text ": the import comes from the Python standard library (e.g., "
│     │       ├─2 inlineCode "import os"
│     │       └─3 text ").\nThis is easy too: we include a list of all known standard library modules in Ruff itself, so it's\na simple lookup."
